uch that the multiplicative subgroup\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA=\\\\{(a^{2^s})^i:1\\\\le i\\\\le \\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a^{2^s})\\\\}\\\\subset \\\\mathbb{F}_p^*\\n\\\\]\\n\\nhas the following properties:\\n\\n(a) \\\\(|A|>cp/(\\\\log p)^3\\\\); and  \\n(b) \\\\(A\\\\cdot A+A\\\\cdot A=A+A\\\\ne \\\\mathbb{F}_p\\\\).\\n\\nRemark 3.11. In this situation, \\\\(A=A\\\\cdots A\\\\), but, as \\\\(|A\\\\cdot A|\\\\) is typically much larger than \\\\(|A|\\\\), we state it in the form above.\\n\\nLet us mention a related result by Alon and Bourgain [1]: there is an absolute constant \\\\(c>0\\\\) so that there are infinitely many primes \\\\(p\\\\) and a multiplicative subgroup \\\\(A\\\\subset \\\\mathbb{F}_p^*\\\\), with \\\\(|A|\\\\ge cp^{1/3}\\\\), such that there are no \\\\(x,y,z\\\\in A\\\\) with \\\\(x+y=z\\\\).\\n\\n4. Proofs\\n\\nWe observe that Erdős’s conjecture only makes a statement about the number of residue classes of the form \\\\(r^i\\\\) \\\\((\\\\bmod\\\\ p)\\\\) (for fixed \\\\(r\\\\) and \\\\(p\\\\)), but not about its algebraic structure. Observing that these classes are a multiplicative subgroup of \\\\(\\\\mathbb{F}_p^*\\\\), we now study sumsets of multiplicative subgroups in \\\\(\\\\mathbb{F}_p^*\\\\). In recent years, there has been a considerable number of papers concerned with sumsets modulo primes. Of the many results available, we choose those results that seem best suited for our application.\\n\\n4.1. Lemmas. In the proof, we use the first two lemmas below coming from multiplicative number theory and several very recent quantitative versions of results from additive combinatorics.\\n\\nLemma 4.1 (Erdős-Murty [17, Theorem 5], Pappalardi [37]). Let \\\\(\\\\Gamma\\\\subset \\\\mathbb{Q}^*\\\\) be a multiplicative subgroup of rank \\\\(d\\\\). Suppose that \\\\(\\\\Gamma\\\\) is generated by the mutually coprime numbers \\\\(b_1,\\\\ldots,b_d\\\\). For all primes \\\\(p\\\\) not dividing the denominators of \\\\(b_1,\\\\ldots,b_d\\\\), we define \\\\(f_\\\\Gamma(p)\\\\) to be the order of \\\\(\\\\Gamma\\\\) \\\\((\\\\bmod\\\\ p)\\\\). Let \\\\(\\\\varepsilon(x)\\\\) be any function tending to zero as \\\\(x\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). For all but \\\\(o(x/\\\\log x)\\\\) primes \\\\(p\\\\le x\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_\\\\Gamma(p)\\\\ge p^{d/(d+1)+\\\\varepsilon(p)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1017/S000497271600023X Published online by Cambridge University Press--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nMultiplicative subgroups are not restricted sumsets (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/410931178_Multiplicative_subgroups_are_not_restricted_sumsets)\\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; This gives a sharp, complete resolution of the restricted-sumset analogue of the generalized Sárközy conjecture over prime fields. ... ozy conjecture over prime ... The present paper concerns the analogous question for restricted sumsets.\\n\\n# Multiplicative subgroups are not restricted sumsets\\n\\n  * July 2026\\n\\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2607.25711\\n\\nAuthors:\\n\\nChi Hoi Yip\\n\\n  * Hong Kong University of Science and Technology\\n\\nSemin Yoo\\n\\n  * Institute for Basic Science\\n\\nImage\\n\\nDownload file PDFRead file\\n\\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nWe determine exactly which proper multiplicative subgroups of a prime field can be represented as a restricted sumset of the form $A\\\\mathbin{\\\\widehat{+}} A=\\\\{a+a':a,a'\\\\in A,\\\\ a\\\\ne a'\\\\}$. We prove that a proper multiplicative subgroup $H\\\\le\\\\mathbb F_p^*$ cannot satisfy $H=A\\\\mathbin{\\\\widehat{+}} A$ whenever $|H|\\\\ge7$, and that this threshold is sharp. In fact, such a decomposition exists precisely when $|H|\\\\in\\\\{1,3,6\\\\}$, and we classify all decompositions in these exceptional cases. This gives a sharp, complete resolution of the restricted-sumset analogue of the generalized Sárközy conjecture over prime fields. This significantly extends and refines previous results of Shkredov and Yip.\\n\\nContent uploaded by Chi Hoi Yip\\n\\nAuthor content\\n\\nAll content in this area was uploaded by Chi Hoi Yip on Jul 29, 2026\\n\\nContent may be subject to copyright.\\n\\nMULTIPLICATIVE SUBGROUPS ARE NOT RESTRICTED SUMSETS\\n\\nCHI HOI YIP AND SEMIN YOO\\n\\nA BS TR ACT. We determine exactly which proper multiplicative subgroups of a prime ﬁeld can\\n\\nbe represented as a restricted sumset of the form A b\\n\\n+A={a+a′:a, a′∈A, a =a′}. We prove\\n\\nthat a proper multiplicative subgroup H≤F∗\\n\\np cannot satisfy H=A b\\n\\n+A whenever |H| ≥ 7, and\\n\\nthat this threshold is sharp. In fact, such a decomposition exists precisely when |H|∈{1,3,6},\\n\\nand we classify all decompositions in these exceptional cases. This gives a sharp, complete\\n\\nresolution of the restricted-sumset analogue of the generalized S´\\n\\nark¨\\n\\nozy conjecture over prime\\n\\nﬁelds. This signiﬁcantly extends and reﬁnes previous results of Shkredov and Yip.\\n\\n1. I NTRODUCTION\\n\\nThroughout the paper, let p be a prime and let F∗\\n\\np=F p\\\\ {0}. For a divisor d>1 of p−1,\\n\\nwrite\\n\\nS d={x d:x∈F∗\\n\\np}, M =|S d|=p−1\\n\\nd.\\n\\nThus every proper multiplicative subgroup of F∗\\n\\np is of the form S d.\\n\\nA long-standing theme in arithmetic combinatorics is to determine whether a set with strong\\n\\nmultiplicative structure can admit a nontrivial additive decomposition. A celebrated conjec-\\n\\nture of S´\\n\\nark¨\\n\\nozy [14] asserted that, for every sufﬁciently large prime p, the subgroup S 2 of\\n\\nnonzero quadratic residues cannot be written as S 2=A+B with |A|,|B| ≥ 2. The prob-\\n\\nlem was resolved through three distinct breakthroughs. Hanson and Petridis [8] proved the\\n\\nconjecture for almost all primes using a Stepanov-type polynomial method, and Kalmynin [9]\\n\\nsubsequently settled the remaining primes. Most recently, Rudnev and Tyrrell [13] classiﬁed\\n\\nall decompositions S d=A+B for proper multiplicative subgroups of prime ﬁelds: apart\\n\\nfrom a singleton summand, the only possibility is |S d|= 4 and |A|=|B|= 2. Related\\n\\nadditive and multiplicative decomposition problems have been studied extensively; see, for ex-\\n\\nample, [14,19,15,17,18,10,11].\\n\\nThe present paper concerns the analogous question for restricted sumsets. For A⊂F p, write\\n\\nA b\\n\\n+A={a+a′:a, a′∈A, a =a′}.\\n\\nRestricted sumsets are classical objects in additive combinatorics. The Erd ˝\\n\\nos–Heilbronn prob-\\n\\nlem asks for the smallest possible size of A b\\n\\n+A; its sharp prime-ﬁeld form was proved by\\n\\nDias da Silva and Hamidoune [4], with a later polynomial-method proof by Alon, Nathanson,\\n\\nand Ruzsa [1]. They also arise naturally in problems where every pair of distinct elements is\\n\\nOne of our main\\n\\nstructural results shows that, once M≥4, such a representation forces all |A|\\n\\n2sums of distinct\\n\\npairs to be different. Thus a hypothetical set A must exhibit simultaneously the additive rigidity\\n\\nof a Sidon set and the multiplicative symmetry of a subgroup.\\n\\nThis leads to the following restricted-sumset analogue of the generalized S´\\n\\nark¨\\n\\nozy conjecture.\\n\\nConjecture 1.1. Let d≥2. If p≡1 (mod d)is sufﬁciently large, then there is no set A⊂F p\\n\\nsuch that A b\\n\\n+A=S d.\\n\\nFor d= 2, Shkredov [16] proved the conjecture over prime ﬁelds for p > 13. His Fourier-\\n\\nanalytic argument uses identities speciﬁc to the quadratic character to force any such set A to be\\n\\na perfect difference set, and then excludes this rigid possibility using the multiplier theorem and\\n\\nfurther character-sum identities. These ingredients are particular to the quadratic setting and do\\n\\nnot readily extend to general ﬁnite ﬁelds or to subgroups of higher index.\\n\\nYip [20, Theorem 1.1] subsequently extended the quadratic case to every ﬁnite ﬁeld F q of\\n\\nodd order q > 13. His proof combined Stepanov’s method with arithmetic input: over ex-\\n\\ntension ﬁelds, the resulting numerical constraint led to a special case of the Nagell–Ljunggren\\n\\nequation [3], while for higher-index subgroups, Kummer’s theorem and a uniform-distribution\\n\\nresult of Bergelson et al. [2] were used to control the exceptional primes. More precisely, for\\n\\neach ﬁxed d≥3, Yip proved the conjecture for a set of primes of lower relative density at least\\n\\n3/4 among the primes p≡1 (mod d)[20, Theorems 1.2 and 1.4]. He also proved that, when\\n\\nM≥3,if S d=A b\\n\\n+A and either |A|is odd or 0∈A, then A is Sidon and\\n\\n2 A∩S d=∅, M =|A|\\n\\n2.\\n\\nThe case where |A|is even and 0/∈A remained open, as did inﬁnitely many possible charac-\\n\\nteristics in the higher-index problem.\\n\\nWe settle the problem completely over prime ﬁelds.\\n\\nTheorem 1.2. Let d≥2, and let p≡1 (mod d)be a prime with p≥7 d+ 1. Then there is no\\n\\nset A⊂F p such that A b\\n\\n+A=S d.\\n\\nSince p−1 = dM, the hypothesis in Theorem 1.2 is exactly M=|S d| ≥ 7. The result is\\n\\ntherefore uniform in both the characteristic and the index, has no asymptotic qualiﬁcation, and\\n\\ngives the best possible threshold. Indeed, together with the analysis of M≤6 in Remark 6.1,\\n\\nit yields a complete classiﬁcation: a proper multiplicative subgroup S d can be represented as\\n\\nA b\\n\\n+A if and only if M∈ {1,3,6}. For M= 1, the decompositions are the two-element sets\\n\\nA={x, 1−x}with x=1\\n\\n2; for M= 3, the unique decomposition is A=−S d; and for M= 6,\\n\\n2\\n\\nthe two decompositions are A={0}⊔H and A={0}⊔(−H), where H is the subgroup of\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAn optimal lower bound for the size of the restricted sumsets containing powers (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022314X25002574)\\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; For an integer $r\\\\ge 3$, let $\\\\mathcal{p}(r)$ be the least prime with $\\\\mathcal{p}(r)\\\\nmid r$ and $\\\\mathcal{q}(r)$ be the least prime with $\\\\mathcal{q}(r)\\\\nmid r$ such that r is not a primitive root of $\\\\mathcal{q}(r)$. ... Then$$|{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+\\\\cdots +{A}_{h}|\\\\ge \\\\mathrm{min}\\\\{p,\\\\sum _{i=1}^{h}|{A}_{i}|-h+1\\\\}. ... Let A be a finite set of integers with $|A|\\\\ge 3$, $\\\\mathrm{gcd}A=1$, $A\\\\subseteq [0,n]$ and $0,n\\\\in A$.\\n## Section snippets\\n\\n### Proof of Theorem 1.1\\n\\nFor an integer $r\\\\ge 3$, let $\\\\mathcal{p}(r)$ be the least prime with $\\\\mathcal{p}(r)\\\\nmid r$ and $\\\\mathcal{q}(r)$ be the least prime with $\\\\mathcal{q}(r)\\\\nmid r$ such that r is not a primitive root of $\\\\mathcal{q}(r)$. Let$$\\\\mathcal{U}=\\\\{k\\\\in \\\\mathbb{N}:k\\\\equiv 0\\\\phantom{\\\\rule{0.40em}{0ex}}(\\\\mathrm{mod}\\\\phantom{\\\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}6),\\\\phantom{\\\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}k\\\\equiv 0,2,3\\\\phantom{\\\\rule{0.40em}{0ex}}(\\\\mathrm{mod}\\\\phantom{\\\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}5),\\\\phantom{\\\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}k\\\\equiv 0,3,5\\\\phantom{\\\\rule{0.40em}{0ex}}(\\\\mathrm{mod}\\\\phantom{\\\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}7)\\\\}.$$\\n\\nLemma 2.1\\n\\n([23]) Let $r\\\\ge 2$ be an integer.\\n\\n(i) $r\\\\notin \\\\mathcal{U}$ if and only if $3\\\\le m(r)\\\\le 8$;\\n\\n(ii) If $r\\\\in \\\\mathcal{U}$, then $m(r)=\\\\mathrm{min}\\\\{2\\\\mathcal{p}(r),\\\\mathcal{q}(r)\\\\}$.\\n\\nLemma 2.2\\n\\n(See [14, Theorem 2.3].) Let $h\\\\ge 2$ be an integer. Let p be a prime number, and ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},\\\\dots ,{A}_{h}$ be nonempty subsets of $\\\\mathbb{Z}/p\\\\mathbb{Z}$. Then$$|{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+\\\\cdots +{A}_{h}|\\\\ge \\\\mathrm{min}\\\\{p,\\\\sum _{i=1}^{h}|{A}_{i}|-h+1\\\\}.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA proof of the Freiman-Lev conjecture (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/411714725_A_proof_of_the_Freiman-Lev_conjecture)\\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Keywords: restricted sumsets; Freiman-Lev conjecture; inverse problem ... Freiman’s 2 A theorem has continued to attract signiﬁcant attention in number theory ... It is natural to study the lower bound for the cardinality of the restricted sumset 2∧A.\\n\\n# A proof of the Freiman-Lev conjecture\\n\\n  * August 2026\\n\\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2608.04760\\n\\n  * License\\n  * CC BY 4.0\\n\\nAuthors:\\n\\nYujie Wang\\n\\nYujie Wang\\n\\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\\n\\nMin Tang\\n\\nMin Tang\\n\\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\\n\\nImage\\n\\nDownload file PDFRead file\\n\\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nLet $A=\\\\{a_{0}, a_{1}, \\\\ldots, a_{k-1}\\\\}$ be a set of $k>7$ integers such that $0=a_{0}<a_1<\\\\cdots<a_{k-1}$ and $\\\\gcd(A)=1$. The set $2^{\\\\wedge}A=\\\\{a+b: a, b\\\\in A, a\\\\neq b\\\\}$ is called the restricted sumsets of A. Freiman-Lev conjecture is a well-known conjecture which related to restricted sumsets [V.F. Lev, Restricted set addition in groups, I. The classical setting, J. London Math. Soc. 62(2000), 27-40]. Up to now, Freiman-Lev conjecture is still open for all $a_{k-2}\\\\geqslant 2k-4$ and $a_{k-1}\\\\geqslant 2k-2$. In this paper, we complete the proof of the Freiman-Lev conjecture by resolving this final and most challenging case.\\n\\nA proof of the Freiman-Lev conjecture⋆\\n\\nYujie Wang a, Min Tang a,∗\\n\\na School of Mathematics and Statistics, Anhui Normal University, Wuhu 241002, P. R. China\\n\\nAbstract\\n\\nLet A={a 0, a 1, . . . , a k−1}be a set of k > 7 integers such that 0 = a 0< a 1<··· < a k−1\\n\\nand gcd(A)=1. The set 2∧A={a+b:a, b ∈A, a =b}is called the restricted sumsets of\\n\\nA. Freiman-Lev conjecture is a well-known conjecture which related to restricted sumsets\\n\\n[V.F. Lev, Restricted set addition in groups, I. The classical setting, J. London Math. Soc.\\n\\n62(2000), 27-40]. Up to now, Freiman-Lev conjecture is still open for all a k−2⩾2 k−4\\n\\nand a k−1⩾2 k−2. In this paper, we complete the proof of the Freiman-Lev conjecture\\n\\nby resolving this ﬁnal and most challenging case.\\n\\nKeywords: restricted sumsets; Freiman-Lev conjecture; inverse problem\\n\\n2020 MSC: 11B13\\n\\n1. Introduction\\n\\nLet A be a set of integers and let gcd(A)be the greatest common divisor of all nonzero\\n\\nelements of A. We deﬁne the sumsets of A and the restricted sumsets of A to be\\n\\n2 A={a+b:a, b ∈A},2∧A={a+b:a, b ∈A, a =b},\\n\\nrespectively. Write [a, b] = {x∈Z|a⩽x⩽b}.\\n\\nIn 1959, G.A. Freiman [3] obtained the famous Freiman’s 2 A theorem.\\n\\nTheorem A. Let A⊆[0, l]be a set of k⩾3 integers such that 0, l ∈A,gcd(A)=1.\\n\\nThen\\n\\n|2 A|⩾l+k if l⩽2 k−3,\\n\\n3 k−3 if l⩾2 k−2.\\n\\nFreiman’s 2 A theorem has continued to attract signiﬁcant attention in number theory\\n\\nand combinatorics, the reader can see [1, 2, 5].\\n\\nIt is natural to study the lower bound for the cardinality of the restricted sumset 2∧A.\\n\\nHowever, the problems of the restricted sumsets demand analyzing and handling of all\\n\\npossible subtle structures which makes the problems far more complex than dealing with\\n\\ngeneral sumsets. Early work on restricted sumsets can be found in, e.g., Nathanson’s\\n\\ninverse theorems for subset sums [7]. Nevertheless, it was not until 1999 that G. A.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nPingzhi Yuan - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/pingzhi-yuan)\\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Critical Restricted Sumsets at the Boundary $\\\\lvert A\\\\rvert+\\\\lvert B\\\\rvert=p$ ... We determine when either set is an arithmetic progression and compute exact difference-set cardinalities. ... The index conjecture in zero-sum theory asserts that if $(n,6)=1$, then every minimal zero-sum sequence of length four over $C_n$ has index one. ... This proves the length-four index conjecture for every $n$ coprime to $6$.\\n\\nBrowse math.CO papers from arXiv\\n\\n## Papers by Pingzhi Yuan\\n\\n12 paper(s) by this author · All BibTeX\\n\\n2026-09-13\\n\\nHongjian Li, Yangcheng Li, Pingzhi Yuan\\n\\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\\n\\nCritical Restricted Sumsets at the Boundary $\\\\lvert A\\\\rvert+\\\\lvert B\\\\rvert=p$\\n\\nLet $p$ be an odd prime, and write $A\\\\RS B=\\\\{a+b:a\\\\in A,\\\\ b\\\\in B,\\\\ a\\\\ne b\\\\}$. We classify all pairs $A,B\\\\subseteq\\\\Fp$ satisfying $|A|+|B|=p$, $|A|>|B|=\\\\ell\\\\ge1$, and $|A\\\\RS B|=p-2$. After normalizing the two missing sums to $\\\\{0,1\\\\}$, we obtain exactly $\\\\ell+1$ explicit models. The classification forces $B\\\\subseteq A$ and yields, for fixed $p,\\\\ell$, exactly $\\\\binom p2(\\\\ell+1)$ ordered pairs and $1+\\\\lfloor\\\\ell/2\\\\rfloor$ equivalence classes under simultaneous affine transformations. We determine when either set is an arithmetic progression and compute exact difference-set cardinalities. For $\\\\ell\\\\ge3$ and $p\\\\ge4\\\\ell-5$, the cardinality $|B-B|$ determines the equivalence class at fixed $p,\\\\ell$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nTopics in arithmetic combinatorics (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/390746429_Topics_in_arithmetic_combinatorics)\\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; A conjecture of S´ark¨ozy [89] asserts that if p is a suﬃciently large prime, then the set of non-zero ... a restricted sumset analogue of S´ark¨ozy’s conjecture over all ﬁnite ﬁelds with an odd characteristic. ... objects, restricted sumsets appear frequently in the study of additive number theory and additive\\nWe denote S d(F q) = {x d:x∈F∗\\n\\nq}to be the\\n\\nsubgroup of F∗\\n\\nq with order q−1\\n\\nd. If q is assumed to be ﬁxed, for brevity, we simply writ